l  demand is soaring many people are  falling through the cracks and have  little to no financial aid  the spanish food bank federation's  latest figures show just how many people  now depend on their help  in the first part of the pandemic we had  one million beneficiaries  now we're looking at one million eight  hundred and ninety thousand  yes  their warehouse on the outskirts of  madrid supplies seventy thousand kilos  of food  every day that's mainly paid for by  government and eu  aid these schemes have been extended and  a recent campaign for donations should  ensure that the warehouse doesn't run  out of stock  back at the church father gonzalo needs  more volunteers to fill the many  trolleys and reduce waiting times  they go from empty to full within  seconds before they get to the waiting  hungry  i confess that on some days during the  pandemic i thought i'd not make it home  i was walking at the speed of a person  who had aged 40 years  but that doesn't stop him one full  trolley contains food supplies for a  month  including treats that families cannot  now afford  when the children see the cocoa or  chocolates they're very happy  because i can only buy the essentials  the pandemic has hit spain's already  batted economy hard  it's unlikely it will recover quickly  enough so people  may have to rely on food handouts for  some time to come  now as we saw in the report there the  coronavirus pandemic has pushed many  around the world  into economic hardship now we want to  look at the link  between the coronavirus and social  inequality  here to help shed light on that is nico  dragano he is a professor from the  institute of medical sociology  at heinrich haines university in  dusseldorf  welcome to you now your research has  focused on how social phenomena like  poverty  affect one on a biological level what  has the coronavirus taught you about  covid19 and existing economic divides  it shows us that even  the corona pandemic has a social  inequalities  dimension when we look at the  epidemiological  data we see that  poorer people or poorer countries or  poorer  areas face a higher burden  of infections and we also performed some  studies on severity of disease and found  that poor people have a higher risk  to have a severe cause of the disease  when getting infected so  we clearly see a social gradient in the  corona pandemic  so basically we're looking at a  situation where if you're poor  you're already more at risk for the  virus and if you do get the virus you  may face  even more economic pain is there a  health policy answer to that kind of  vicious circle  so that's a hard to answer because  this is a structural problem we see  health inequalities  in nearly each disease we studied so we  see it in cardiovascular diseases  we see it in psychological diseases  it's always there and we haven't found a  proper  response yet i guess it's uh quite  important to notice that and to think  strategically  how can we handle this problem in the  acute pandemic in the situation we are  into now but we need a long-term  strategy  to reduce health inequalities in  all diseases in the long run  but given these long existing  inequalities in society  how would a strategic handling that you  speak of  how would that look like in practical  terms  so i guess at the who the world health  organization  gave us some good advices  so there's a strategy called health and  all policies  that means that the country should  implement  ideas and strategies to improve health  and all the policies  and in particular to think about health  inequalities in this context  for instance when you look at  educational policies or  tax policies where the root of the  inequalities lie you should consider  that  and you need to implement  this kind of thinking and this kind of  strategy  in all the policies which shape the  social determinants of health so there  are some ideas but we need to implement  them  can you give us any examples of 